<section class="section-sm">
  <h2>What students learn</h2>
  <p>Running a Project Broadsheet publication teaches skills that transfer directly to professional newsrooms and digital media careers:</p>
  <ul class="feature-block__list" style="margin-top: var(--space-lg);">
    <li><strong>Version control</strong> — Every article is a Git commit. Students learn to track changes, revert mistakes, and collaborate without overwriting each other's work.</li>
    <li><strong>Structured content</strong> — Front matter, editorial sections, tags, and series teach students to think about content as data — the same mental model used in professional CMS environments.</li>
    <li><strong>Journalistic best practices</strong> — The corrections system, source documents collection, and response tracking are built into the platform. Using them correctly is part of the workflow, not an afterthought.</li>
    <li><strong>Web publishing</strong> — Students deploy to a real domain with HTTPS, configure SEO metadata, submit to Google News, and manage RSS feeds — practical skills most journalism programs don't teach.</li>
    <li><strong>Accessibility and privacy</strong> — The framework targets WCAG 2.2 AA and ships with cookieless analytics. Students learn these aren't optional features; they're built in.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

